---
export const theme = {
  id: "summer25",
  displayName: "Summer 2025",
  notchLabel: "Sommar 25",
  status: "Active",
  notchColor: "#dfeef2",
  slug: "summer-2025",
  summary: "Mid-June to mid-July rainforest + snorkeling theme constraints.",
};

const siteTitle = `Theme: ${theme.displayName} – La Familia`;
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{siteTitle}</title>
    <style>
      :root {
        color-scheme: light;
        --text: #1f2933;
        --muted: #5b6570;
        --border: #d7dce1;
        --surface: #ffffff;
        --accent: #2f5d7c;
        --background: #f6f7f9;
      }

      * {
        box-sizing: border-box;
      }

      body {
        margin: 0;
        font-family: "Inter", "Segoe UI", system-ui, -apple-system, sans-serif;
        color: var(--text);
        background: var(--background);
      }

      main {
        max-width: 960px;
        margin: 0 auto;
        padding: 64px 24px 104px;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        gap: 32px;
      }

      h1,
      h2 {
        margin: 0 0 12px;
        font-weight: 600;
        letter-spacing: -0.02em;
      }

      h1 {
        font-size: 2.4rem;
        line-height: 1.2;
      }

      h2 {
        font-size: 1.1rem;
        letter-spacing: -0.01em;
        color: var(--text);
      }

      p {
        margin: 0 0 16px;
        line-height: 1.6;
        color: var(--text);
      }

      ul {
        margin: 0 0 16px;
        padding-left: 18px;
        line-height: 1.6;
      }

      .section {
        background: var(--surface);
        border: 1px solid var(--border);
        border-radius: 12px;
        padding: 28px 32px;
      }

      .subtitle {
        color: var(--muted);
        font-size: 1.02rem;
        max-width: 720px;
      }

      .note {
        color: var(--muted);
        font-size: 0.98rem;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <section class="section">
        <h1>Theme: {theme.displayName}</h1>
        <p>
          Summer-2025 is a clearly defined travel window and theme rather than an open-ended
          exploration.
        </p>
        <p>
          The journey is planned for mid-June through mid-July, with a total duration of
          approximately two weeks. Timing is flexible within this window, but seasonal conditions
          are not. Destinations must function during this period, particularly with regard to sea
          conditions and access to nature.
        </p>
        <p>
          The thematic focus is deliberate and narrow: tropical rainforest and snorkeling. Both
          elements must be present in a meaningful way. Neither serves as a backdrop.
        </p>
        <p>
          By rainforest, we mean actual tropical rainforest or jungle — dense, humid, biologically
          rich environments where the landscape feels continuous rather than fragmented. We are not
          looking for isolated green pockets, landscaped nature or plantations presented as jungle.
          The experience should feel immersive, not symbolic.
        </p>
        <p>By snorkeling, we mean reliable, rewarding snorkeling:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>living coral, with realistic expectations</li>
          <li>sufficient visibility</li>
          <li>manageable currents</li>
          <li>conditions that are viable in June–July</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          We are not divers, and we are not interested in destinations where snorkeling is
          secondary, occasional or dependent on rare weather windows. The reef experience should be
          an integrated part of the stay, not an exception.
        </p>
        <p>
          This combination significantly limits the number of suitable destinations during the
          European summer. We accept that limitation and treat it as a filter rather than a problem.
          Fewer options with higher confidence are preferred over broader but compromised choices.
        </p>
        <p>
          The trip should follow a structured and secured framework. Key elements — flights, main
          accommodation, primary transfers and major segments — are ideally planned and booked in
          advance. This provides stability and allows the experience itself to unfold without
          constant logistical decisions.
        </p>
        <p>Within that framework, we value authenticity and variation:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>traditional or locally rooted accommodation for part of the trip</li>
          <li>food settings that reflect local culture rather than international polish</li>
          <li>environments that feel lived-in rather than curated</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          These elements may exist within the same location or be spread across different parts of
          the journey.
        </p>
        <p>
          Well-known places or landmarks are not excluded by default. If something is culturally
          significant, it can be included. However, if similar experiences exist in less touristic
          forms nearby, those are generally preferred.
        </p>
        <p>
          The journey may include a short urban stopover, such as Singapore or Dubai, either at the
          beginning or end of the trip. Such a stop should serve a clear purpose: logistical
          clarity, contrast, recovery from long-haul travel, or a controlled transition between
          environments.
        </p>
        <p>
          As a family with teenagers, the destination must feel safe, calm and socially comfortable.
          Backpacker-party environments are a negative. Visible social distress or exploitation is
          something we consciously avoid.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rain is acceptable. Persistent strong winds, rough seas or conditions that consistently
          undermine snorkeling are not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where possible, we prefer to stay together as a group rather than split across multiple
          accommodations. On return journeys involving long transfers, a final night close to the
          departure airport is considered a sensible buffer rather than a compromise.
        </p>
        <p>
          Summer-2025 is not about maximizing variety or distance. It is about finding one coherent
          setting where rainforest and reef coexist in a way that genuinely works during this
          specific time of year, within a structure that feels secure, calm and intentional.
        </p>
      </section>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
